Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 19:44:01 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 078/154] refactor(chrome): route surface messages through a
 table, and settle six sidebar faults
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

One commit because the window file carries all of them and its arms
were moved while the other edits landed beside them.

THE ROUTER. OnSurfaceMessage was 804 lines: 68 if-arms answering 69
names, a 20-name overview forwarder and the capture verb parser. The
arms are now one handler each, bound by a table of 90 rows in a second
TU of the window, and SurfaceMessageRouter maps a name to its handler.
Each handler carries its arm verbatim, redundant name check included, so
the two arms one name had keep their order. A browser test walks the
table against kSurfaceMessages. The window file went from 10054 lines
to 9388 and its function to a Route call plus the verb fallback.

THE SIDEBAR WIDTH has one owner. The window's cached copy drifted from
the core's store and from the page three separate ways, and one writer
never wrote the store at all, so a Cmd+S restore after a drag brought
back the pre-drag width. The window reads the store now.

A FAVOURITE'S ROW is hidden by the core. The live strip marks a tab a
tile has claimed, the core turns that into the row's token and skips it
in both the model build and every visible-to-persisted mapping, so the
two index spaces agree - which is what erasing the row in C++ broke.

THE ROWS ANNOUNCE THEMSELVES. The page sends sidebarRowsDrawn after
each render and the window logs it, so a capture can wait on the line
instead of on a settle that caught an empty sidebar five runs in six.

THE HOTKEY PATH SAYS WHAT IT DID. A chord that reaches the window logs
matched, fired, or declined by which gate. With the sidebar's render
widget holding first responder, Cmd+S fires; the recorded miss was the
harness dropping the chord, not the responder.

TWO BROWSER TESTS SKIP with a reason where the dylib is deployed, and
the negative half moves to a unit test in a binary nothing starts the
context in.

Signed-off-by: János Mikó <693558+janosmiko@users.noreply.github.com>
